					<h1 class="entry-title">AC and Furnace Replacement: Should You Replace Both Systems at the Same Time?</h1><p class="et_pb_title_meta_container"><span class="published">Sep 30, 2026</span> | <a href="/heating-ventilation-and-air-conditioning/" rel="category tag">Heating Ventilation And Air Conditioning​</a></p>
				</div>
				<div class="et_pb_title_featured_container"><span class="et_pb_image_wrap"><img loading="lazy" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="2240" height="1260" src="/wp-content/uploads/AC-and-Furnace-Replacement-Should-You-Replace-Both-Systems-at-the-Same-Time-1.webp" alt="AC and Furnace Replacement Should You Replace Both Systems at the Same Time (1)" title="AC and Furnace Replacement Should You Replace Both Systems at the Same Time (1)" srcset="/wp-content/uploads/AC-and-Furnace-Replacement-Should-You-Replace-Both-Systems-at-the-Same-Time-1.webp 2240w, /wp-content/uploads/AC-and-Furnace-Replacement-Should-You-Replace-Both-Systems-at-the-Same-Time-1-1280x720.webp 1280w, /wp-content/uploads/AC-and-Furnace-Replacement-Should-You-Replace-Both-Systems-at-the-Same-Time-1-980x551.webp 980w, /wp-content/uploads/AC-and-Furnace-Replacement-Should-You-Replace-Both-Systems-at-the-Same-Time-1-480x270.webp 480w" sizes="(min-width: 0px) and (max-width: 480px) 480px, (min-width: 481px) and (max-width: 980px) 980px, (min-width: 981px) and (max-width: 1280px) 1280px, (min-width: 1281px) 2240px, 100vw" class="wp-image-20251114167424" /></span></div>
			</div><div class="et_pb_module et_pb_post_content et_pb_post_content_0_tb_body blog-post-content">
				
				
				
				
				<p>AC and <a href="/heating-service/">furnace replacement</a> at the same time usually makes sense when both systems are 10 years old or more, or when one has failed and the other is the same age. You get equipment that’s matched, one labor bill instead of two, and the efficiency rating you paid for. But if your <a href="/furnace-repair-texas-handbook/">furnace is young and healthy</a>, keep it. A new AC can often be paired with it just fine.</p>
<p>That’s the short answer. The longer one depends on what’s sitting in your attic right now.</p>
<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Your furnace and AC share the indoor coil and the blower, so they work as one system.</li>
<li>A <a href="/air-conditioning-installation/">new AC</a> on an old blower often falls short of its rated efficiency.</li>
<li>If both units are <a href="/ac-replacement-frisco-tx/">10 to 15 years old</a>, replacing them together is usually the better deal.</li>
<li>A furnace under 10 years old with a compatible blower can usually stay.</li>
<li>Not sure where yours stands? Air Zone Experts will look at both systems and tell you straight whether the furnace needs to go. Call (214) 430-9059.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why Your Furnace and AC Work as One System</h2>
<p>Most homes in Little Elm, Frisco, and the rest of North Texas have a split system. The condenser sits outside. The furnace sits in the attic, the garage, or a closet. They look like two separate machines. They aren’t.</p>
<p>Start with the evaporator coil, the part that actually cools your air. It sits right on top of the furnace (or beside it). When the outdoor unit gets replaced, that coil nearly always goes too, since it has to match the new condenser’s size and refrigerant.</p>
<p>Then there’s the blower inside the furnace. It pushes air across the coil and through your ducts, and it runs all summer, not just in winter. In a DFW house where the AC runs hard from May through September, that blower may have more hours on it than you’d guess. One that was sized for a 15-year-old AC may not move enough air for a new system.</p>
<p>Refrigerant is the third piece. Newer air conditioners use A2L refrigerants such as R-454B instead of R-410A, and an old coil built for R-410A generally won’t work with a new outdoor unit.</p>
<p>All of this is why efficiency ratings like SEER2 are certified for matched sets of equipment. Put a new condenser on mismatched indoor parts and the number on the sticker may not be the number you get.</p>
<h2>When to Replace AC and Furnace Together</h2>
<p>Age is the big one. If the furnace and AC went in together 12 or 15 years ago, they’re wearing out on the same clock. Replace only one, and there’s a good chance you’ll be calling for the second install a year or two later.</p>
<p>Timing matters too. Say your AC quits in July and the furnace is the same age. The crew is already there. The coil is already coming out. Pricing both at that moment costs you nothing, and it may save you a second tear-out.</p>
<p>Some homeowners want the comfort upgrade. A variable-speed AC or heat pump does its best work with a variable-speed blower, so upgrading only the outdoor unit leaves a lot of that benefit behind. If you’ve been living with hot upstairs bedrooms, weak airflow, or sticky humidity, an aging blower could be part of the reason.</p>
<p>And sometimes the furnace just isn’t safe. Rust, a cracked heat exchanger, or ignition that keeps failing are reasons to replace it no matter what the AC is doing. If that’s where you are, here’s what to expect from a <a href="/heating-service/">furnace replacement</a> and how the process works.</p>
<h2>When Replacing Only One System Is Fine</h2>
<p>You don’t always need both, and an honest contractor will say so.</p>
<p>Gas furnaces in Texas tend to outlast air conditioners. Our winters are mild, so the furnace simply runs fewer hours. If yours is under 10 years old, in good shape, and has a blower that can handle the new AC, pairing it with a new condenser and matched coil is usually a sound choice.</p>
<p>The reverse works too. If your AC is only a few years old and the furnace dies, a furnace-only replacement is often reasonable, as long as the new blower is sized to the AC you already have.</p>
<p>Money is a fair reason on its own. If replacing both would stretch the budget too far, replace what failed and plan for the other. Just make sure your technician confirms that the new equipment and the old will actually work together. Compatibility is the whole ballgame here.</p>
<h2>The Cost Side: One Project vs. Two</h2>
<p>Doing both at once costs more on the day you sign. Over a few years, it’s often cheaper. The labor to pull old equipment, set new equipment, and start up the system happens once. So do the permit and the inspection. You also avoid buying a coil now and then reworking that same setup when the furnace gets replaced later. And both warranties start on the same date, which keeps future service simple.</p>
<p>One change worth knowing about: the federal 25C Energy Efficient Home Improvement Credit expired on December 31, 2025, so HVAC equipment installed in 2026 doesn’t qualify for it. Utility and state rebates may still apply, and they’re worth a look.</p>
<p>If the up-front number is what’s holding you back, <a href="/financing/">HVAC financing options</a> with low monthly payments can make a full replacement easier to manage than two surprise bills a year apart.</p>
<h2>Questions to Ask Before You Decide</h2>
<p>Before you sign off on an AC and furnace replacement, ask these:</p>
<p>1. <strong>How old is each system, and what shape is it in?</strong> The technician should check the heat exchanger, blower, and coil, not only the unit that broke.</p>
<p>2. <strong>Will my current furnace work with the new AC?</strong> Ask about blower capacity and the matched coil by name.</p>
<p>3. <strong>Did you do a load calculation?</strong> A system that’s too big or too small wastes energy and wears out early.</p>
<p>4. <strong>What does each option cost over five years?</strong> Compare one combined job against two separate ones.</p>
<p>5. <strong>What’s the warranty?</strong> Get parts and labor coverage in writing.</p>
<p>If a contractor answers all five clearly and doesn’t steer you toward the priciest package, you’ve probably found a good one.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>My HVAC company says to replace my 12-year-old furnace when I install the new AC. Is that necessary or an upsell?</h3>
<p>It depends on condition, not age alone. Plenty of 12-year-old furnaces still have a sound heat exchanger and a blower that can handle a new AC. Ask the contractor to show you the actual problem, whether that’s rust, a weak blower, or a compatibility limit. No clear answer? Get a second opinion.</p>
<h3>Both my furnace and AC are 18 years old and still work fine. Should I replace them now or wait until they break?</h3>
<p>Waiting is a fair choice if you keep up with yearly maintenance and set money aside. The catch is timing. Systems that age tend to fail at the worst moment, like a 105-degree week in August, and then you’re choosing under pressure. Planning ahead lets you pick the equipment, the install date, and the financing on your terms.</p>
<h3>Will a new AC work with my old furnace?</h3>
<p>Sometimes. The new AC needs a matched indoor coil, and your furnace blower has to move enough air for the new system’s size. Because of the refrigerant change, the old coil usually can’t stay. A technician can check the blower capacity and cabinet size and tell you whether the pairing will work.</p>
<h3>Should I repair my AC now and wait to replace both systems when the furnace goes?</h3>
<p>That can work if the repair is affordable and the rest of the AC is solid. Weigh the repair cost against the unit’s age. Putting serious money into an AC that’s already 12 or older is often money better spent on a combined replacement.</p>
<h3>My furnace is only 3 years old. Do I need to replace it when I get a new AC?</h3>
<p>Usually not. A furnace that young has plenty of life left, and most newer models pair fine with a new AC and matched coil. Your technician should still confirm the blower is sized for the new system so you get the efficiency you’re paying for.</p>
<h2>Get an Honest Answer for Your Home</h2>
<p>Sometimes the right call is a full system. Sometimes it’s a new AC on a furnace that has years left in it. Air Zone Experts is family-owned, veteran-owned, and NATE-certified, and the team recommends only what your home needs.</p>
